1
00:00:00,770 --> 00:00:05,030
This is Hireline, an evidence-first recruiting workspace. The goal is to take a

2
00:00:05,030 --> 00:00:09,290
candidate from a resume to a recorded hiring decision, while keeping the recruiter

3
00:00:09,290 --> 00:00:13,550
in control. This recording uses a fresh, isolated database with fictional candidates. No

4
00:00:13,550 --> 00:00:17,810
personal resumes, real credentials, or external accounts are involved. The public dashboard is

5
00:00:17,810 --> 00:00:22,070
read only. It brings together job openings, the current hiring funnel, evidence-backed candidate

6
00:00:22,070 --> 00:00:26,330
matches, and upcoming interviews. The sample workspace contains three software roles and twenty

7
00:00:26,330 --> 00:00:30,590
synthetic resumes. A match percentage describes documented evidence against a particular job rubric.

8
00:00:30,590 --> 00:00:34,850
It is not a prediction of job performance, and it never makes a

9
00:00:34,850 --> 00:00:39,110
hiring decision.

10
00:00:40,810 --> 00:00:45,440
After signing in, the recruiter creates an opportunity for a Python platform engineer.

11
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:50,070
A useful screening process starts with explicit expectations. We enter the responsibilities, select

12
00:00:50,070 --> 00:00:54,700
the hiring manager, and define weighted criteria for Python, Fast A P I,

13
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:59,330
PostgreSQL, and Kubernetes. The weights total one hundred percent. The application can draft

14
00:00:59,330 --> 00:01:03,960
criteria from a description, but a person must check and confirm the rubric

15
00:01:03,960 --> 00:01:08,590
before screening is available. This keeps a job description from becoming an unexplained

16
00:01:08,590 --> 00:01:13,220
scoring formula. Criteria have versions. If the recruiter changes the rubric later, previous

17
00:01:13,220 --> 00:01:17,850
results remain visible, but are marked as stale and require an explicit rescreen.

18
00:01:19,540 --> 00:01:23,780
Next, we import two synthetic resumes, one PDF and one Word document. Uploads

19
00:01:23,780 --> 00:01:28,020
are limited to small batches, and the recruiter explicitly confirms that the information

20
00:01:28,020 --> 00:01:32,260
is fictional. File signatures and size limits are checked at the boundary. Parsing

21
00:01:32,260 --> 00:01:36,500
happens in a durable background task rather than holding open a long request.

22
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:40,750
Progress is visible in the interface. A readable document becomes a candidate profile

23
00:01:40,750 --> 00:01:44,990
and a job application. Those are separate records, so the same person can

24
00:01:44,990 --> 00:01:49,230
be considered for more than one role. This demo deliberately does not perform

25
00:01:49,230 --> 00:01:53,470
optical character recognition; scanned or unreadable documents produce a useful correction message.

26
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:59,180
We can now open the extracted candidate profile. Skills, experience, location, notice period,

27
00:01:59,180 --> 00:02:03,200
and salary expectations are visible in one place. Notice period and salary are

28
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:07,210
explicit information, not hidden components of the skills score. Original documents remain private

29
00:02:07,210 --> 00:02:11,230
and can be downloaded by authorized staff. If extraction makes a mistake, the

30
00:02:11,230 --> 00:02:15,250
recruiter can correct the profile. A correction increments its version, preserving older screening

31
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:19,260
results and making it clear when a fresh evaluation is needed. The resume

32
00:02:19,260 --> 00:02:23,280
text remains the source of evidence; editing a profile does not silently invent

33
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:27,290
a new source quotation.

34
00:02:28,990 --> 00:02:33,380
Starting a screening runs a bounded workflow: evaluate the confirmed criteria, validate the

35
00:02:33,380 --> 00:02:37,770
evidence, calculate the score, and pause for human review. In this offline demonstration

36
00:02:37,770 --> 00:02:42,150
the result is explicitly labelled Demo matcher. A configured Gemini run is a

37
00:02:42,150 --> 00:02:46,540
separate mode and records its model provenance. For every criterion, the interface shows

38
00:02:46,540 --> 00:02:50,930
whether evidence is supported, partial, or insufficient. Accepted quotations must actually appear in

39
00:02:50,930 --> 00:02:55,320
the extracted document at the stated source segment. Unsupported citations cannot contribute to

40
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:59,700
the score. The weighted calculation is performed by application code, using full credit,

41
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:04,090
half credit, or zero. Missing evidence is shown as an open question, rather

42
00:03:04,090 --> 00:03:08,480
than being presented as proof that a candidate lacks a skill.

43
00:03:10,180 --> 00:03:14,240
The workflow is now waiting for a recruiter. We inspect the supporting excerpts,

44
00:03:14,240 --> 00:03:18,310
note the Kubernetes evidence gap, and record a review. LangGraph checkpoints preserve this

45
00:03:18,310 --> 00:03:22,370
pause in persistent storage, so a process restart does not depend on keeping

46
00:03:22,370 --> 00:03:26,430
a browser tab open. Approval acknowledges that a person reviewed the screening. It

47
00:03:26,430 --> 00:03:30,500
does not shortlist, reject, or contact the candidate. We make the next pipeline

48
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:34,560
change separately and document the reason. This separation is visible in the product:

49
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:38,620
recommendations support a decision, while stage changes are deliberate staff actions. It also

50
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:42,690
leaves an audit trail of what happened and when.

51
00:03:44,390 --> 00:03:48,510
A resume cannot answer every question. The recruiter can prepare a short assessment

52
00:03:48,510 --> 00:03:52,630
containing multiple choice and written-response questions. The question editor includes the answer key

53
00:03:52,630 --> 00:03:56,750
or review rubric, and a person approves the content before an invitation is

54
00:03:56,750 --> 00:04:00,870
generated. Multiple choice questions are scored against their keys. Short answers receive advisory

55
00:04:00,870 --> 00:04:04,990
feedback and still require human review. These assessment results remain separate from resume

56
00:04:04,990 --> 00:04:09,110
screening scores. We create an invitation and copy the candidate link. Tokens are

57
00:04:09,110 --> 00:04:13,230
stored as hashes, expire after seven days, and permit one submission. If an

58
00:04:13,230 --> 00:04:17,350
unused link is lost or expires, an authorized recruiter can replace it, immediately

59
00:04:17,350 --> 00:04:21,480
invalidating the previous link.

60
00:04:23,170 --> 00:04:27,720
The candidate sees only the invited questionnaire, not the staff dashboard, answer keys,

61
00:04:27,720 --> 00:04:32,280
recruiter notes, or other candidates. Here we answer the two technical questions and

62
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:36,830
describe a fictional engineering example. We also provide an optional interview availability window.

63
00:04:36,830 --> 00:04:41,380
The interface states the device timezone, and the server stores offset-aware timestamps. Availability

64
00:04:41,380 --> 00:04:45,930
is a preference, not a confirmed booking. Before submitting, the candidate acknowledges that

65
00:04:45,930 --> 00:04:50,490
this is a fictional portfolio demonstration and that the form can be submitted

66
00:04:50,490 --> 00:04:55,040
only once. The receipt confirms successful recording. Revisiting the link shows that receipt

67
00:04:55,040 --> 00:04:59,590
again, while a duplicate submission is rejected by an atomic server-side state transition.

68
00:05:01,290 --> 00:05:05,300
Back in the recruiting workspace, the submitted responses and multiple choice result are

69
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:09,320
available to authorized reviewers. Written-answer feedback is clearly advisory. The recruiter reads the

70
00:05:09,320 --> 00:05:13,330
responses against the approved rubric and records their own notes. This makes the

71
00:05:13,330 --> 00:05:17,340
review inspectable instead of collapsing everything into an unexplained overall score. Candidate invitation

72
00:05:17,340 --> 00:05:21,360
access is deliberately narrow, and submitted answers do not appear in the public

73
00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:25,370
demo. The assessment review also does not automatically advance the application. The team

74
00:05:25,370 --> 00:05:29,380
can use the new evidence to decide whether an interview is worthwhile, while

75
00:05:29,380 --> 00:05:33,390
retaining the original screening and its review history.

76
00:05:35,090 --> 00:05:39,090
We choose the candidate's submitted availability and confirm an interview. In live configuration,

77
00:05:39,090 --> 00:05:43,080
the backend checks Google Calendar before creating an event, uses a stable action

78
00:05:43,080 --> 00:05:47,080
identifier, and restricts attendees to owned test accounts. This recording is in preview

79
00:05:47,080 --> 00:05:51,070
mode, so the visible receipt is a preview, not a claim that an

80
00:05:51,070 --> 00:05:55,070
external event or email was delivered. After the conversation, a hiring team member

81
00:05:55,070 --> 00:05:59,070
records concrete, job-relevant feedback. Finally, the recruiter records the fictional hiring outcome with

82
00:05:59,070 --> 00:06:03,060
a decision note. The complete journey is now visible: an approved rubric, a

83
00:06:03,060 --> 00:06:07,060
parsed resume, source-backed screening, a reviewed assessment, an interview receipt, and a human

84
00:06:07,060 --> 00:06:11,060
decision.

85
00:06:12,750 --> 00:06:17,440
The workspace has three staff roles. An administrator manages staff access and integration

86
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:22,140
configuration. Recruiters manage jobs, candidates, communication approvals, and stage changes. Hiring managers see

87
00:06:22,140 --> 00:06:26,830
assigned jobs and can contribute feedback, but they cannot make recruiter-only changes. These

88
00:06:26,830 --> 00:06:31,520
boundaries are checked in Fast A P I, not merely by hiding a

89
00:06:31,520 --> 00:06:36,210
button. We switch to a manager account to show the reduced controls. Processing

90
00:06:36,210 --> 00:06:40,910
history exposes task outcomes and errors. Safe failed tasks can be retried, while

91
00:06:40,910 --> 00:06:45,600
ambiguous outbound delivery requires reconciliation rather than a blind resend. This matters because

92
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:50,290
a workflow checkpoint alone cannot guarantee an external side effect happens exactly once.

93
00:06:51,980 --> 00:06:56,170
Analytics separates the current stage distribution from historical reach. The funnel shows where

94
00:06:56,170 --> 00:07:00,350
applications are now. Applications reaching each stage measures recorded visits as a percentage

95
00:07:00,350 --> 00:07:04,540
of all applications; skipped stages are not guessed. Timing comes from recorded stage

96
00:07:04,540 --> 00:07:08,720
transitions, and failures remain visible. The project also includes an evaluation harness comparing

97
00:07:08,720 --> 00:07:12,910
the deterministic matcher with a keyword baseline. On the included synthetic dataset, both

98
00:07:12,910 --> 00:07:17,090
methods reach the same top-five relevance results for these three jobs. That is

99
00:07:17,090 --> 00:07:21,280
useful evidence of the demo's behavior, not proof that an advanced model is

100
00:07:21,280 --> 00:07:25,470
better. Extraction, skill recall, and quotation validity are reported separately, with the dataset's

101
00:07:25,470 --> 00:07:29,650
limitations.

102
00:07:31,350 --> 00:07:36,510
The architecture is a focused modular application. React and TypeScript provide the workspace

103
00:07:36,510 --> 00:07:41,680
and candidate forms. Fast A P I owns business access, validation, and permissions.

104
00:07:41,680 --> 00:07:46,840
SQLAlchemy and Alembic manage relational data and migrations. A single worker processes leased

105
00:07:46,840 --> 00:07:52,010
tasks, while LangGraph checkpoints preserve screening progress and approval pauses. Local development uses

106
00:07:52,010 --> 00:07:57,180
SQLite and private local files. The production path uses PostgreSQL, Supabase authentication, and

107
00:07:57,180 --> 00:08:02,340
private storage. Google Calendar, Resend, and Gemini are explicit integration adapters. Completed extraction

108
00:08:02,340 --> 00:08:07,510
and matching results are persisted to avoid unnecessary calls. No Redis, Celery cluster,

109
00:08:07,510 --> 00:08:12,680
vector database, or microservice fleet is required for this scope. The tests cover

110
00:08:12,680 --> 00:08:17,840
permissions, duplicate requests, recovery, evidence validation, and the actual browser journey.

111
00:08:19,540 --> 00:08:23,930
This version is designed for a strictly free portfolio project. Free hosting can

112
00:08:23,930 --> 00:08:28,320
sleep, so queued processing resumes after the service wakes; scheduled reminders are outside

113
00:08:28,320 --> 00:08:32,720
this release. Synthetic data is a deliberate boundary for unpaid hosted A I.

114
00:08:32,720 --> 00:08:37,110
Live integrations require actual owned accounts, configured credentials, and account-level smoke tests before

115
00:08:37,110 --> 00:08:41,500
they can be described as verified. The repository includes setup and deployment guides,

116
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:45,900
database migrations, sample resumes, automated tests, and measured evaluation output. This recording demonstrates

117
00:08:45,900 --> 00:08:50,290
the real local application from intake through outcome, while labelling demo matching and

118
00:08:50,290 --> 00:08:54,680
preview actions honestly. The result is an inspectable recruiting workflow with a clear

119
00:08:54,680 --> 00:08:59,070
path to a small, carefully evaluated live pilot.
